    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and it passes. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the GOLD membership tier rule with minimal structural impact, following the codebase's existing transient-field pattern (the same one used for `namesakeCount` and `bulkSignupWarning`):\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `@Transient householdSize` field with a simple getter/setter, keeping each new method at complexity 1.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a single line that stores the post-create household size (existing same-household owners in the already-loaded `owners` collection, plus the new owner), reusing the existing `HouseholdMatcher.sameHousehold` helper rather than adding new counting logic.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 extended the one `membershipTier` expression line to prefer `\"GOLD\"` when `householdSize >= 3`, otherwise falling through unchanged to the existing SILVER/BRONZE decision.\n\nNo new classes, no changes to `addOwner`'s branching structure beyond the one predicate, and the mapper edit stays a single line. The three touched files each get small, cohesive changes, and the GOLD determination is computed once at create time (where the household collection is already in scope) and read back at GET time via the persisted-in-session transient field \u2014 exactly how the existing tier inputs already flow.\n\nThe test `coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold` passes (the third shared-household member returns `GOLD`), and the full suite is green.",
